#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Dispatch a Code or Merge Review to `codex exec`, and validate the report
it hands back rather than trusting a clean exit code.

    claude-codex-review --base <ref> --repo <path> -o <report.json>
                        [--plan <plan file> | --merge-with <ref>]
                        [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --plan-review <plan file> --repo <path> -o <report.json>
                        [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --design-review <design> <requirements> [<file> ...]
                        --repo <path> -o <report.json> [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --architecture-review <brief> --moment design|final
                        --repo <path> -o <report.json> [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --resume <session id> --answers <file> --round 2|3
                        --moment design|final --repo <path> -o <report.json>
                        [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --check-brief <brief>
    claude-codex-review --usage [--sessions-root <path>]
    claude-codex-review --validate-report <report.json> --round 1|2|3
                        --moment design|final

Arguments are parsed before any stdin access, like the other tools here:
this tool is run by hand, and an unconditional stdin read hangs the one
invocation that matters. In practice this file never reads its own
`sys.stdin` at all -- the diff it generates goes to the *child* process's
stdin, on a file handle this process opens itself, never on fd 0.

`--plan-review` and `--design-review` dispatch a document gate instead of a
diff gate: the wrapper builds its own stdin from the named file(s) via
`merge_brief_lib`, sends the matching fixed brief (`plan-brief.md` /
`design-brief.md`) as the positional, and runs the same stale-report
removal, schema validation and semantic validation as the diff gates below.
Neither takes `--base` or `--merge-with`, and the two document flags are
mutually exclusive -- every such conflict fails at argument parsing (exit 2)
before anything is dispatched.

**Code Review runs on a detached snapshot of HEAD, never the live checkout**:
the wrapper adds a detached worktree at HEAD's commit, diffs and dispatches
there, and removes it afterwards, so uncommitted edits in `--repo` are never
reviewed.

**`--merge-with <ref>` reviews a merge result, not the unmerged branch.** It
fetches `<ref>` from `origin`, merges it into a scratch worktree (in a repo
with no `origin` remote at all, the local branch `<ref>` is merged and nothing
is fetched; a configured `origin` that fails to fetch still refuses), and
sends the reviewer
TWO labelled blocks on stdin instead of one: the branch's own diff, and
what moved on `<ref>` since the branch diverged. A three-dot diff is
invariant under merging the base in (byte-identical), so the diff alone
cannot distinguish the two gates -- the second block is what does.

Each gate runs on a pinned model (see `codex_review_lib`), which the
`codex_review.models.<gate>` key of the toolkit config overrides.
The five architecture-family modes (`--architecture-review`, `--resume`,
`--check-brief`, `--usage`, `--validate-report`) take at most one of
themselves and none of the modes above; they are checked first in `main()`.
`--architecture-review` dispatches the fixed `architecture-brief.md` with the
brief on stdin (read-only, `max` effort, schema-forced, on the
`architecture` gate's model) and prints the Codex
session id so a `needs_context` report can be continued with `--resume`,
which sends only the answers (stdin is /dev/null) back into that session.

Behaviour ladder (see the plan for the full ordering):

Exit codes: 0 report accepted; 1 review failed or refused; 2 usage or
configuration error; 4 Codex unavailable, nothing reviewed.

Behaviour ladder:

  1. Preflight: `codex` on PATH and its auth file readable, else exit 4
     (Codex unavailable); `review-brief.md` present and non-empty, and
     `AGENTS.md` present and non-empty in `--repo` -- absent or blank is a
     refusal, not a warning. Then the toolkit config is read and each gate's
     model resolved; an unreadable config or a bad `codex_review.models`
     value exits 2 before anything is dispatched.
  2. Headroom -- the ONLY non-fatal step. Printed always; never blocks.
  3. Capture ids and build the scratch worktree, diff gates only. Resolve
     `HEAD`, `--base` (or `origin/<ref>` after the fetch) and the merge base
     to immutable shas ONCE, before anything is built -- nothing after this
     reads them from `--repo` again. A Code Review `--base` naming a branch
     `origin` tracks is fetched first, and refused (nothing dispatched)
     when the local branch is strictly behind `origin` and HEAD already
     carries commits it lacks (see `_refresh_code_review_base`). A dirty
     `--repo` prints a stderr warning counting the uncommitted paths the
     review does not see (never a refusal). Then build a detached scratch
     worktree: at HEAD for Code Review (a snapshot), or at HEAD with
     `origin/<ref>` merged in for Merge Review -- the branch point is
     captured BEFORE merging (merging destroys it). A conflict aborts the
     merge, removes the worktree, and exits non-zero -- never review a
     conflicted tree, and never silently fall back to the unmerged branch.
  4. Generate the diff(s) in the scratch worktree. Code Review:
     `git diff <base sha>...<head sha>`, one diff on stdin, unlabelled.
     Merge Review: `=== YOUR BRANCH ===` (`diff <origin sha>...<head sha>`)
     and `=== MOVED UNDER YOU SINCE ===` (`diff <branch point> <origin
     sha>`), both on stdin. An unresolvable ref or an EMPTY branch diff are
     both loud failures -- an empty diff dispatches a reviewer with nothing
     to read, which returns a clean-looking `APPROVE, findings: []` that
     nothing else in this pipeline would catch. An EMPTY upstream block is
     different: it is legitimate (nothing landed on `<ref>` since the
     branch diverged), so it prints a sentence saying so and continues
     rather than failing. Then, on both diff gates, print one
     `reviewed snapshot: head=… tree=… base=…@… merge-base=…` line -- the
     record of what was reviewed, outside the model's report.
  5. Dispatch `codex exec` in the foreground, `cwd` AND `-C` both set to
     the scratch worktree for both diff gates (and to `--repo` for the
     document gates), diff piped explicitly on stdin (never inherited).
  6. Non-zero exit from `codex` -> loud failure. If its stderr says the
     model is not supported for this account, exit 4 instead, and leave no
     report behind.
  7. The `-o` file must exist, be non-empty, and parse as JSON.
  8. It must validate structurally against the schema.
  9. It must validate SEMANTICALLY -- a structurally valid report can still
     describe a review that never happened.
  10. Per-run cost, from the run's OWN token counts rather than the
      integer meter delta (the latter quantises to a whole point and is
      noise for one run): `run_cost` attributes a rollout file to this
      dispatch by cwd and start time, reports its uncached input tokens
      and, where a fit exists, the estimated % of the window spent. Then
      ONE fresh meter snapshot, printed as a gates-left line -- never step
      2's snapshot path, which is now stale.
  11. Print the report location and a one-line finding count.
  12. Cleanup of the scratch worktree: `git worktree remove --force`,
      ALWAYS on every catchable exit -- including on every failure path
      from step 4 onward, not just a merge conflict at step 3. SIGTERM
      counts: a handler turns it into `SystemExit(143)`, which kills the
      codex child and runs the cleanup. SIGKILL skips it.
"""

